The thousands digit has $5$ odd choices ($1,3,5,7,9$) and $4$ even choices ($2,4,6,8$); each other digit has $5$ odd and $5$ even choices.
\begin{itemize}
\item Thousands digit odd: one more of the other three digits is odd ($3$ ways to choose which), so $5\cdot3\cdot5^3=1875$.
\item Thousands digit even: two of the other three digits are odd ($3$ ways), so $4\cdot3\cdot5^3=1500$.
\end{itemize}
Total $1875+1500=\boxed{3375}$.

question:
Let $ABCD$ be a trapezoid with bases $AB$ and $CD$ ($AB\parallel CD$, $AB\ne CD$), and let $AB=a$, $BC=b$, $CD=c$, $DA=d$, $AC=m$ and $BD=n$. Suppose that $m^2+n^2=(a+c)^2$.
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] Prove that the lines $AC$ and $BD$ are perpendicular.
\item[(b)] Prove that $ac<bd$.
\end{enumerate}

solution:
\textbf{(a)} Through $C$ draw the line parallel to $BD$; it meets line $AB$ at $K$. Since $DC\parallel BK$ and $CK\parallel DB$, the quadrilateral $DBKC$ is a parallelogram, so $BK=DC=c$ and $CK=DB=n$. Because $\overrightarrow{AB}$ and $\overrightarrow{DC}$ point the same way, $K$ lies on ray $AB$ beyond $B$ and $AK=a+c$.

