The reflection in the axis of symmetry of the triangle (the altitude from $A$) swaps $B$ and $C$ and swaps the two trisectors, so it swaps $D$ and $E$. Hence $BE=CD=5$, $BC=11$ and $AE=AD$.

In triangle $AEC$, the segment $AD$ bisects $\angle EAC$ (it splits it into two of the three equal parts of $\angle A$). By the angle bisector theorem,
\[
\dfrac{AE}{AC}=\dfrac{ED}{DC}=\dfrac{1}{5},\qquad\text{so } AC=5\,AE .
\]
Let $h$ be the height from $A$; its foot is the midpoint of $BC$ and $ED$, which is at distance $\dfrac{1}{2}$ from $E$ and $\dfrac{11}{2}$ from $C$. So
\[
AE^2=h^2+\dfrac{1}{4},\qquad AC^2=h^2+\dfrac{121}{4},\qquad AC^2-AE^2=30 .
\]
With $AC^2=25AE^2$ this gives $24AE^2=30$, $AE^2=\dfrac{5}{4}$, and
\[
AC^2=\dfrac{125}{4},\qquad AC=\boxed{\dfrac{5\sqrt5}{2}}.
\]
(Then $h=1$; the triangle is very flat, with $\angle A\approx159.6^\circ$.)

